        students who you worked with on the assignment.
        Don't forget to put your name at the top of the first page!
    </p>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the model
                <md>
                    B(t)=\frac{10}{50e^{-t}+1}.
                </md>
                Here <m>B(t)</m> gives the population of bunnies (in hundreds)
                at year <m>t</m> (in months).
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write code to plot the function and comment on what you can
                    extrapolate from the graph.
                    What is the end behavior?
                    What does that represent for this problem?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Vary the carrying capacity (the 10 in the above function) by
                    making it a parameter, say <m>k</m>.
                    Let the carrying capacity be <m>k = 5, 10, 20, 25</m>.
                    What is the effect of changing the carrying capacity?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write code (you can copy and modify your code from above) in
                    order to see what happens when you change the 50 in the
                    denominator.
                    Let's call this a new parameter <m>z</m>.
                    Let's leave the carrying capacity at <m>k = 10</m> and now
                    vary the value of <m>z</m>.
                    Plot <m>B(t)</m> with four different values of <m>z</m>.
                    What is the effect of changing this parameter?
                    Based on your observation, what might this parameter be
                    measuring?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now, let's test the sensitivity of these parameters.
                    First, let <m>k</m> vary by <m>2\%</m>
                    <m>{(k = 9.6, 9.8, 10, 10.2, 10.4)}</m> with <m>z=50</m> and
                    measure the change in <m>B(4)</m>. 
                    Based on your results, do you think the model is sensitive
                    to the parameter <m>k</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now, let <m>k=10</m> and vary <m>z</m> by <m>2\%</m>
                    <m>{(z = 48, 49, 50, 51, 52)}</m>.
                    Measure the change in <m>B(4)</m>.
                    Based on your results, do you think the model is sensitive
                    to the parameter <m>k</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>